---
source: cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: "e.run(&[\"--help\"], \"\")"
---
exit: 0
--- stdout
Tijori turns a USB drive into an encrypted vault that opens on macOS, Linux and Android.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 other error, 2 wrong password/keyfile/phrase, 3 tampered, 4 damaged, 5 needs a
newer Tijori, 6 locked by another writer.

Usage: tijori [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  init         Create a vault on a drive
  shell        Unlock once and run several commands; lock on exit
  mount        Show the vault as a folder in Finder or your file manager until you press Ctrl+C,
               eject it, or leave it idle
  ls           List a folder
  put          Add files and folders (recursively) to a vault folder
  get          Copy files and folders out of the vault into a local folder
  mkdir        Create a folder
  mv           Move or rename a file or folder
  rm           Delete a file, or a folder with -r
  cat          Print a file to standard output
  passwd       Change the password of a password slot
  keyslot      List, add or remove keyslots
  rekey        Re-encrypt everything under a new master key. Old copies of the header, and every old
               password, stop working
  verify       Check every hash and decrypt every chunk
  gc           Delete encrypted objects nothing refers to any more
  backup       Copy the whole vault, still encrypted, to another folder or drive. Run it again to
               bring the copy up to date; only changes are copied
  info         Show format version, settings, keyslots and revision
  settings     Show or change vault settings
  restore      Copy a backup onto a drive, so the drive holds the backup's state. Only what differs
               is copied
  completions  Print a completion script for your shell, for example `tijori completions zsh >
               ~/.zfunc/_tijori`
  break-lock   Remove a lock left behind by a Tijori that is no longer running
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --vault <PATH>
          The drive, or its TIJORI folder. Found automatically when exactly one vault is mounted

      --with-recovery
          Unlock with the 24-word recovery phrase instead of the password

      --with-keyfile <PATH>
          Unlock a password + keyfile slot with this keyfile

      --password-stdin
          Read passwords, phrases and confirmations from standard input, one per line, instead of
          prompting on the terminal

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
--- stderr
